---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Kinetic sand online is a pile you drag in the browser. This page is a column mound, not a
      GPU demo. Drag through it. Grains settle. Reset pile restores a centred heap when the sand
      packs into a corner.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example: Input: a finger on a 4:3 stage. Output: a displaced pile that settles, with no
      account and no install. Feel intensity picks column count so a budget phone can keep up.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Kinetic Sand →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="what-is">
    <h2>What is kinetic sand in a browser? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Physical kinetic sand is a mouldable heap. Virtual sand is the same urge as a drag. Mobile
      games want an install. WebGL demos drop frames on a low-end phone. This pile is a grid of
      heights, cheap enough to run in a tab.
    </p>
    <p>
      Depth is height plus a short shadow, not colour alone, so a low-vision user can still see
      a ridge. Pinch does not zoom the page: the canvas captures the pointer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How does the sand pile work? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The stage is a row of columns. A drag lowers heights under the pointer and spills a little
      to the sides. Then a settle pass lets steep slopes fall. Therefore the pile looks like it
      flows without running a particle ocean.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, on low intensity there are 24 columns. High uses 56. Settings → Feel intensity
      is the quality control, so a budget phone can stay on low. However, reduced motion still
      lets you dig: grains jump to rest instead of trickling.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="reset">
    <h2>Why is there a Reset pile control? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Drag enough and the heap packs into a corner. Other toys leave you there with no next
      action, so people reload and break the loop. Reset restores the centred mound, keeps focus,
      and does not reload assets. That continuation is the craft of this tool.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Reloading a packed pile">
      <p>
        A refresh drops Feel prefs from the session even if they persist, and it costs a second.
        Reset pile is the digital scoop back to the middle.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Quality is intensity, not a third slider">
      <p>
        Low, medium, and high under Settings → Feel change how many columns the pile uses. A
        Quality dropdown would duplicate a preference every fidget already shares.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Reloading a packed corner throws away a second and feels like the toy ended. Reset pile is
      the scoop back to the middle. Another mistake is expecting a GPU demo: this is a column
      pile on purpose, so a phone-width tab stays usable.
    </p>
    <p>
      Judging depth by colour only fails when contrast is low. Height plus a short shadow is the
      ridge. Therefore a packed edge is still readable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="vs">
    <h2>Kinetic sand vs alternatives <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Mobile sand games want an install. WebGL sand looks richer and then drops frames. Slime is
      pull instead of a pile. Pop It is taps. Pick kinetic sand when the gesture is a drag through
      a heap, in the browser, with a reset.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="play">
    <h2>How do I play kinetic sand on a phone? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The stage is sized for a phone-width column. Play hides the rest of the page so the pile
      fills the screen. Reset stays in the corner because packing is how the task ends. Exit or
      Escape restores the page. Reduced motion still lets you dig; grains jump to rest.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="try">
    <h2>Try the pile <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Open the tool, drag through the mound, then Reset pile when it packs. Mute if you need the
      room quiet. Nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Kinetic Sand →</a>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
